 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Everyone knows Anna Sui loves to do research. She was especially energized this season, because she was introduced to an artist she hadn’t heard of before: <a href=\"https://newportartmuseum.org/exhibitions/howard-gardiner-cushing-a-harmony-of-line-and-color/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Howard Gardiner Cushing</a>. She made this discovery quite by chance while flipping through a 1972 issue of <em>Vogue</em> (<a href=\"https://www.vogue.com/slideshow/see-the-1972-vogue-story-that-inspired-anna-sui-s-dreamy-spring-2027-collection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">see it here</a>) while looking for something specific and altogether different. Sui lives for “those moments when you discover something and it just takes you down a rabbit hole and you just can’t stop; you just keep going, going, going. I love that,” she said. “When you learn something new, you learn a new universe almost, and that’s what happened when I saw these pictures.”</p><p>The pictures in question are of comely water nymphs with jewels in their hair and their dolphin-like attendants. There’s a softness to the paintings, a delicate prettiness that brought to Sui’s mind storybook illustrations by the likes of Edmund Dulac; those lead to the development of a purple and black fairy-tale print. Digging deeper, Sui found drawings inlaid with nacre, which she then connected with pearl-inlaid furniture by Louis Süe. They became the starting point for a camisole and miniskirt adorned with gradient pearly paillettes quarter-sized up top and penny-like below. The parasols were inspired by the <em>pochoirs</em> of Art Deco artist George Barbier; and the lineup also included a “Poiret stripe” pajama set. While specific references are of interest, of course, it’s not replication Sui was after but a kind of vibesmaxxing. This season’s primary sources were from the 1910s and ’20s, but the models projected a ’70s-style softness.</p><p>Sui creates wonderment by blurring and blending her inspirations into something unmistakably hers. “Layering is the important thing in the season,” she said. One of the best examples of such was Look 24, an Empire slip with high side slits worn over a printed mesh top and with rhinestone studded jeans. Like the soundtrack, the ensemble had a touch of grunge.</p><p>Smiling models wafted down the runway in softly colored, flowy pieces. The main story was lingerie dressing, with camis and slips cut in any number of charming fabrics; many were embellished with ribbons and rosettes. Kimono cuts and pajama sets connected the idea of dreaminess with interiority, lounging, and languor.</p><p>In the end, the less layered looks were the most impactful because they let the pieces shine. These included cabochon-embellished jeans worn with an embroidered top; the white eyelet looks, one shown with a bedazzled logo tee; and the closing plissé looks that share the coloring of Cushing’s sea sprite paintings. The black dress with stars at the bust, a ’90s remake, was also notable. It’s no fish tale to say that this was a high watermark of a collection.</p></div>",
